---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { gccRegion } from '../regions/gcc';
import { collection } from '../lib/content';

const countries = gccRegion.countries;
const ports = gccRegion.ports;
const updates = collection('updates')
  .filter((d) => d.title)
  .sort((a, b) => (b.date || '').localeCompare(a.date || ''))
  .slice(0, 5);

const vatLabel = (v: number) => (v === 0 ? 'No VAT' : `${Math.round(v * 100)}% VAT`);
const gateway = (slug: string) => ports.find((p) => p.country === slug);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Freighthub — China to GCC freight intelligence"
  description="Independent China-to-GCC freight analysis: six markets, their ports, customs, VAT, certification and the cost stack behind a landed price."
>
  <h1>China to GCC freight, minus the forwarder's sales pitch</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Freighthub is an independent editorial project about moving goods from China to the six Gulf
    markets. It covers the ports, the customs and certification regimes, the sector economics, and the
    decisions that sit between a factory gate and a Riyadh warehouse. It does not sell freight, book
    containers or take commissions from carriers — which is why the numbers here can be read as
    analysis rather than as a quote.
  </p>

  <h2>Start from the decision you actually have</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/compare/sea-vs-air/">Sea or air?</a></h3>
      <p>Transit time against freight cost per kilo, and the shipment sizes where the answer flips.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/ports/">Which gateway?</a></h3>
      <p>Jebel Ali, Jeddah, Dammam, Hamad, Sohar, Shuwaikh and the inland pivots behind them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/countries/">Which market?</a></h3>
      <p>Six countries, six customs regimes: duty, VAT, certification and the documents each one asks for.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/guides/certification/">Which certificate?</a></h3>
      <p>SABER and SASO for Saudi, ESMA for the UAE, and the sector rules that decide clearance.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The six markets</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Market</th><th>Currency</th><th>Indirect tax</th><th>Main gateway</th><th>Guide</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {countries.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{c.name}</td>
            <td>{c.currency}</td>
            <td>{vatLabel(c.vat)}</td>
            <td>{gateway(c.slug)?.name ?? '—'}</td>
            <td><a href={`/countries/${c.slug}/`}>Open</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What actually decides landed cost</h2>
  <p>
    On most China–GCC lanes the ocean freight is not the line that breaks a budget. Duty, VAT, the
    certificate that has to exist before a container is released, and the variance in clearance time
    decide more outcomes than the per-container rate does. A forwarder quoting a low freight number
    and leaving the certificate to the buyer is not offering a cheaper route; it is moving the cost to
    a later page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The guides therefore separate the three layers: the freight rate, the statutory charges, and the
    handling that depends on who clears the goods. <a href="/guides/cost-and-transit/">The cost and
    transit guide</a> sets out the stack; <a href="/compare/ddp-vs-self-clearance/">DDP versus
    self-clearance</a> covers the decision most importers get wrong first.
  </p>

  <h2>Latest updates</h2>
  <ul>
    {updates.map((u) => (
      <li>
        <a href={`/updates/${u.slug}/`}>{u.title}</a>
        {u.date ? <span class="meta"> · {u.date}</span> : null}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/updates/">All updates</a> · <a href="/notes/">Editorial notes</a></p>

  <h2>How this is researched</h2>
  <p>
    Every substantive page names its sources, and most of them are primary: ZATCA and the Saudi
    customs portal, SABER and SASO, the UAE Ministry of Economy, DP World and Mawani port notices.
    Where a figure is a range rather than a published tariff, the page says so and shows what moves
    it. Where the position is editorial — which port to prefer for a given cargo, whether DDP is worth
    it — the page says that too.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page sets out the research and correction process; the
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a> records what changed and when; the
    <a href="/about/">about page</a> states the independence position in full. If a page here is
    wrong, the correction is published rather than quietly edited.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
